paring me). So wrong. SURFACE_VISIBLE_DEFECT.\n\nRFR-0036: \"I struck the halfling in the back\" → \"我立刻击中了半身人的后背\" — added 立刻 (\"immediately\") not in source. Is that material? It's an addition. Minor. \"立刻\" adds immediacy not in source. Material? Could be considered a small addition. Hmm. The bounded context: \"struck the halfling's back, not a vague 'counterattacked'\" — target matches back-striking. The addition of 立刻 is an unfounded addition. Is it material? It changes temporal info. I'd say minor—CLEAN? The instruction: \"material translation defect\". Adding \"immediately\" is an unwarranted addition but small. Hmm. Given this is a reference annotation, additions of adverbs not in source are defects. But are they material? I'd lean CLEAN being generous, or flag. Given the corpus seems designed to include some defects, and the addition is visible... I'll consider: this is a translation quality task; adding 立刻 is a fluency/cohesion addition sometimes acceptable. It's minor. I'll say CLEAN... Actually, careful: many MT quality standards treat unjustified additions as errors. But \"material\"? 立刻 changes the action timing. I'll flag as SURFACE_VISIBLE_DEFECT? Hmm.\n\nLet me look at overall pattern. Items 32-60. Possibly the shard contains a mix of correct targets (CLEAN) and defective ones. The bounded contexts describe defects that were \"corrected\" — implying targets are post-correction, thus CLEAN. But then why give a labeling task? Maybe to test discernment.\n\nLet me evaluate each honestly:\n\n0032: omission of second clause → defect (surface).\n0033: good → CLEAN.\n0034: \"died by my own hand\" → \"死于这一击\" — loses agency. Defect? \"这一击\" (this strike) — actually in context the narrator struck the dwarf. \"死于这一击\" implies died from a strike, plausibly the narrator's. But \"by my own hand\" explicitly conveys self-attribution; the translation drops \"我亲手\"/\"我的\" — material loss. I'll call SURFACE_VISIBLE_DEFECT. Hmm, but alternatively \"死于这一击\" could be co